# Normalize line endings so a checkout is usable on every OS.
#
# Why this file exists: `backend/entrypoint.sh` is executed by bash *inside
# the Linux container*, but it is checked out on developer machines. Git
# stores it with LF, and on Windows `core.autocrlf=true` (the default that
# Git for Windows installs) rewrites it to CRLF on checkout. bash then
# reads the trailing \r as part of each command:
#
#   entrypoint.sh: line 2: set: -: invalid option
#   entrypoint.sh: line 3: $'\r': command not found
#   entrypoint.sh: line 26: syntax error: unexpected end of file
#
# The container restart-loops, and `docker compose up` — the single command
# the README promises — never works on a Windows checkout. It is not
# reproducible on macOS/Linux, which is exactly why it survived.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides autocrlf for these paths, whatever the developer's
# global Git config.

# Default: let Git decide what is text, and normalize it in the repository.
* text=auto

# Executed inside Linux containers — must stay LF, always.
*.sh text eol=lf
backend/entrypoint.sh text eol=lf

# Docker tooling reads these; keep them LF for the same reason.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
docker-compose.yml text eol=lf

# Python/Dart run through interpreters that accept either, but keeping the
# repository normalized avoids whole-file diffs when developers on
# different platforms touch the same file.
*.py text eol=lf
*.dart text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf

# Binary: never touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
